        ":question/id": "e735330d-c2f3-5b05-83d4-12a709d794e0",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48507",
        ":question/problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{sin\u2061x}{2x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=sin\u2061x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(sin\u2061x)^{\u2032}\\cdot (2x)-sin\u2061x\\cdot (2x)^{\u2032}}{(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{cos\u2061x\\cdot (2x)-sin\u2061x\\cdot 2}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(xcos\u2061x-sin\u2061x)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xcos\u2061x-sin\u2061x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\pi cos\u2061(\\pi )-sin\u2061(\\pi )}{2(\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)-(0)}{2{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }.$"
      },
