          ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=3 x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}\\cdot (3 x)-(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (3 x)^{\\prime}}{(3 x)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (3 x)-(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (3)}{(3 x)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\left(x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}{9 x^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{3 x^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(\\pi)&=\\frac{\\pi\\cos(\\pi)-\\sin(\\pi)}{3\\pi^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{\\pi(-1)-0}{3\\pi^{2}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3 \\pi}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- \\frac{1}{3 \\pi}.$",
