          ":question/id": "2e9ec61c-a65c-56b3-820e-2d60ba70912c",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-9908",
          ":question/problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"
        },
